{"text": "RJR Interoffice Memorandum\n\nTO: John H. Reynolds\nFROM: David G. Gilbert\n\nRE: Dr. Weinstein\u2019s letter concerning calming effects of smoking.\n\nDATE: May 16, 1984\n\nI would be interested in seeing what research Dr. Weinstein\nmight propose.. He states that he has completed work for another\n\u2018tobacco company and has reason to believe that he can demonstrate\nsmoking to have calming, relaxing effects. My work in this area\nsuggests that snoking has calming effects, but these effects have\nnot been reliable enough to claim than they have been clearly\nestablished. Thus, I am interested in learning more about Dr.\nWeinstein\u2019s approach to this area and would like to have him\nsubmit a proposal on this topic. His publications suggest that\nhe is well qualified to conduct such an investigation.\n\nIn spite of my interest in his ideas, I believe that the best\ninvestment we could make in outside bicbehavioral research vould\nbe in a prospective longitudinal study that includes the\nevaluation of physiological, personality, emotional, genetic, and\nsocial factors in young people prior to and then after the age of\nthe onset of smoking. Such a longitudinal study with repeated\nPhysiological and emotional measures would help to determine not\nonly whether or not smoking has calming effects, but also would\nhelp determine the mechanisms responsible for such effect:\n\nZELZ 8670S",
